{"date": "2026-10-07", "post_id": 1466612, "title": "Supernova Remnant Pa 30", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-7-supernova-remnant-pa-30/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation: </strong>\"Happy New Year!\" No, wait, this is not a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-6-fireworks-vs-supermoon/\">fireworks display</a>. <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noirlab2624a/\">This image</a> shows Nebula <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-april-3-unusual-nebula-pa-30/\">Pa 30</a>, observed with the <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/programs/gemini-observatory/gemini-north/\">Gemini North Telescope</a> in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/hawaii-82975/\">Hawai'i</a>. It is likely the remnant of an old <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-behind-the-discoveries/hubble-stellar-explosions/\">supernova explosion</a>: separate historical records by Chinese, Japanese and Arabic astronomers tell of a \"<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guest_star_(astronomy)\">guest star</a>\" that appeared in the sky for <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/stunning-echo-of-800-year-old-explosion/\">185 days</a> in the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SN_1181\">year 1181</a>. It is believed that this bright new point of light came from the supernova that caused the fireworks in Pa 30. Astronomers <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1598398386929-4d5370672e9f\">don't know exactly</a> what happened in this unusual explosion, classified as a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_Iax_supernova\">Type Iax</a> supernova, but it is thought to be caused by the merger of two <a href=\"https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/objects/dwarfs1.html\">white dwarfs</a>. The <a href=\"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acb138\">mysterious central star</a> in the image is extremely hot and produces a strong wind, possibly forming the <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/news/noirlab2624/\">radial filaments</a>. Look closely at them: those <a href=\"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae9cb2\">pearl-like knots</a> stringing the filaments are 4 <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/voyager-1-what-is-a-light-day/\">light-days</a> in diameter. Understanding how a supernova created this amazing nebula continues an 845-year old mystery (and counting).<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.gemini.edu/\">International Gemini Observatory</a>/<a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/\">NOIRLab</a>/<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a>/<a href=\"https://www.aura-astronomy.org/\">AURA</a>. Acknowledgment: PI: <a href=\"https://sites.harvard.edu/charbonneau/directory/tim-cunningham/\">T. Cunningham</a> (<a href=\"https://www.cfa.harvard.edu/\">Center for Astrophysics - Harvard &amp; Smithsonian</a>). Image Processing: J. Miller &amp; M. Rodriguez (<a href=\"https://www.gemini.edu/\">International Gemini Observatory</a>/<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a> <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/copyright/\">NOIRLab</a>), <a href=\"https://www.uaa.alaska.edu/academics/college-of-arts-and-sciences/departments/physics-and-astronomy/faculty/rector.cshtml\">T.A. Rector</a> (<a href=\"https://www.uaa.alaska.edu/\">University of Alaska Anchorage</a>/<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a> <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/copyright/\">NOIRLab</a>), <a href=\"https://www.demahub.com/about.html\">D. de Martin</a> &amp; <a href=\"https://mahdizamani.com/about\">M. Zamani</a> (<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a> <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/copyright/\">NOIRLab</a>).", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.gemini.edu/\">International Gemini Observatory</a>/<a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/\">NOIRLab</a>/<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a>/<a href=\"https://www.aura-astronomy.org/\">AURA</a>. Acknowledgment: PI: <a href=\"https://sites.harvard.edu/charbonneau/directory/tim-cunningham/\">T. Cunningham</a> (<a href=\"https://www.cfa.harvard.edu/\">Center for Astrophysics - Harvard &amp; Smithsonian</a>). Image Processing: J. Miller &amp; M. Rodriguez (<a href=\"https://www.gemini.edu/\">International Gemini Observatory</a>/<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a> <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/copyright/\">NOIRLab</a>), <a href=\"https://www.uaa.alaska.edu/academics/college-of-arts-and-sciences/departments/physics-and-astronomy/faculty/rector.cshtml\">T.A. Rector</a> (<a href=\"https://www.uaa.alaska.edu/\">University of Alaska Anchorage</a>/<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a> <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/copyright/\">NOIRLab</a>), <a href=\"https://www.demahub.com/about.html\">D. de Martin</a> &amp; <a href=\"https://mahdizamani.com/about\">M. Zamani</a> (<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a> <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/copyright/\">NOIRLab</a>).", "alt": "A red explosion extending radially like a firework over a dark field of stars.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-7-supernova-remnant-pa-30/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/noirlab2624a.jpg?w=3233&h=3429&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 7 - Supernova Remnant Pa 30</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-7-supernova-remnant-pa-30/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Cecilia Chirenti\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: &quot;Happy New Year!&quot; No, wait, this is not a fireworks display. This image shows Nebula Pa 30, observed with the Gemini North Telescope in Hawai&#039;i. It is likely the remnant of an old supernova explosion: separate historical records by Chinese, Japanese and Arabic astronomers tell of a &quot;guest star&quot; that appeared in the sky for 185 days in the year 1181. It is believed that this bright new point of light came from the supernova that caused the fireworks in Pa 30. Astronomers don&#039;t know exactly what happened in this unusual explosion, classified as a Type Iax supernova, but it is thought to be caused by the merger of two white dwarfs. The mysterious central star in the image is extremely hot and produces a strong wind, possibly forming the radial filaments. Look closely at them: those pearl-like knots stringing the filaments are 4 light-days in diameter. Understanding how a supernova created this amazing nebula continues an 845-year old mystery (and counting).Tomorrow&#039;s picture: sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"supernova remnant pa 30\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Supernova Remnant Pa 30\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-7-supernova-remnant-pa-30/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/noirlab2624a.jpg?w=3233&#038;h=3429&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/noirlab2624a.jpg?w=3233&#038;h=3429&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A red explosion extending radially like a firework over a dark field of stars.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-07T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-06T21:33:50-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Supernova Remnant Pa 30\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/noirlab2624a.jpg?w=3233&#038;h=3429&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 7<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/noirlab2624a.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/noirlab2624a.jpg\" alt=\"A red explosion extending radially like a firework over a dark field of stars.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Supernova Remnant Pa 30 </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.gemini.edu/\">International Gemini Observatory</a>/<a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/\">NOIRLab</a>/<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a>/<a href=\"https://www.aura-astronomy.org/\">AURA</a>. Acknowledgment: PI: <a href=\"https://sites.harvard.edu/charbonneau/directory/tim-cunningham/\">T. Cunningham</a> (<a href=\"https://www.cfa.harvard.edu/\">Center for Astrophysics - Harvard &amp; Smithsonian</a>). Image Processing: J. Miller &amp; M. Rodriguez (<a href=\"https://www.gemini.edu/\">International Gemini Observatory</a>/<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a> <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/copyright/\">NOIRLab</a>), <a href=\"https://www.uaa.alaska.edu/academics/college-of-arts-and-sciences/departments/physics-and-astronomy/faculty/rector.cshtml\">T.A. Rector</a> (<a href=\"https://www.uaa.alaska.edu/\">University of Alaska Anchorage</a>/<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a> <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/copyright/\">NOIRLab</a>), <a href=\"https://www.demahub.com/about.html\">D. de Martin</a> &amp; <a href=\"https://mahdizamani.com/about\">M. Zamani</a> (<a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/\">NSF</a> <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/copyright/\">NOIRLab</a>).\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation: </strong>\"Happy New Year!\" No, wait, this is not a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-6-fireworks-vs-supermoon/\">fireworks display</a>. <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noirlab2624a/\">This image</a> shows Nebula <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-april-3-unusual-nebula-pa-30/\">Pa 30</a>, observed with the <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/programs/gemini-observatory/gemini-north/\">Gemini North Telescope</a> in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/hawaii-82975/\">Hawai'i</a>. It is likely the remnant of an old <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-behind-the-discoveries/hubble-stellar-explosions/\">supernova explosion</a>: separate historical records by Chinese, Japanese and Arabic astronomers tell of a \"<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guest_star_(astronomy)\">guest star</a>\" that appeared in the sky for <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/stunning-echo-of-800-year-old-explosion/\">185 days</a> in the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SN_1181\">year 1181</a>. It is believed that this bright new point of light came from the supernova that caused the fireworks in Pa 30. Astronomers <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1598398386929-4d5370672e9f\">don't know exactly</a> what happened in this unusual explosion, classified as a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_Iax_supernova\">Type Iax</a> supernova, but it is thought to be caused by the merger of two <a href=\"https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/objects/dwarfs1.html\">white dwarfs</a>. The <a href=\"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/acb138\">mysterious central star</a> in the image is extremely hot and produces a strong wind, possibly forming the <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/news/noirlab2624/\">radial filaments</a>. Look closely at them: those <a href=\"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ae9cb2\">pearl-like knots</a> stringing the filaments are 4 <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/voyager-1-what-is-a-light-day/\">light-days</a> in diameter. Understanding how a supernova created this amazing nebula continues an 845-year old mystery (and counting).<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-6-a-complete-auroral-oval-from-smile/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nCecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261007/html"}